    <body>
      <div type="clause" n="Prol. 3">
        <head><ref type="lawCode" target="law:XAtr">X Atr</ref> Prol. 3</head>
        <ab type="editionText" xml:lang="ang" source="#robertson" n="§3" corresp="#wit-vr">⁊ þis is seo gerædnes þe we willað healdan, swa swa we æt Eanham fæste gecwædon.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#robertson" corresp="#wit-vr">And this is the ordinance which we desire to observe in accordance with what we have firmly decreed at Enham.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="de" source="#liebermann">Und Folgendes ist der Beschluss, den wir halten wollen, so wie wir zu Enham fest bestimmt haben.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude" ana="#fromGerman">And the following is the ordinance which we will keep, as we firmly decided at Enham.</ab>
        <note type="comparison" source="#liebermann" n="269"><ref target="clause:VI-Atr-Latin-Paraphrase_Prol">VI Atr Lat Prol.</ref></note>
        <note type="comparisonPrinted" source="#liebermann">ebd. Paraph. Prol.</note>
        <note type="editorial">Vr has a cross-reference sign here; four lines in the margin in another hand, each mutilated by four or five letters at the front (‘Godes gife … [Ea]dgares cinges ge … im þe ic Æðel[red] … ealle witan’), were meant to replace the underlined ‘þe wo’; the corrector seems to ascribe the content to Edgar (compare D of I Cn 1). Liebermann compares the Paraphrase of VI Atr, Prol.</note>
      </div>
    </body>
